{"page": 1, "text": "Visible text of EX-99.1_2026-11-03_q32026shareholderletter.htm, sha256 afd6a29f6e2ca989ce6ad1fb25b19f4eedae1fa45c5a96e4ae100aa426dda6e8.\nEX-99.1 2 q32026shareholderletter.htm EX-99.1\nQ3 2026 Shareholder Letter\ninvestors.block.xyz\nTo Our Shareholders At the beginning of this year, we established four principles to guide our work forward:\n\u2022Customers want peace of mind, not more products. \u2022Companies move fast or slow based on information flow. \u2022The best way to integrate machine intelligence is to become it. \u2022Intelligence depends on signal, and money is the most honest signal.\nWe used these principles to refocus how we work, what we work on, and where we're going. And we saw this compound into another strong set of results in the third quarter. We exceeded our guidance with 22% gross profit growth, 92% Adjusted Operating Income growth, and 109% Adjusted Diluted EPS growth, and we raised our guidance across each of these metrics for the year.\nThese principles also led us to refine the master plan we shared at Investor Day at the end of last year:\n\u2022Make our #company an intelligence, and enable others to do the same. \u2022Build the best intelligence to run your #business. \u2022Build the best intelligence to run your #money. \u2022Build open financial #infrastructure for all. \u2022Connect #neighborhoods globally.\nToday we're delivering intelligence to our customers, connecting our ecosystems, and building open financial infrastructure to help achieve our financial results. Square Kiosk gives every restaurant the opportunity to have intuitive physical intelligence in every location, easing the ordering and line burden so they can focus more on their food and experience. Managerbot, which we just rebranded to Squaremates, now has an entirely new architecture and is available to Square sellers in the U.S., giving them access to intelligence and automation tools to help them save time and run their businesses better. We are planning to do the same with Moneybot, to predict and present the right interface to people with all the capabilities Cash App has to offer. We\u2019re connecting our ecosystems in a way we never have before and are already seeing results: Neighborhoods was a key driver of Cash App scaling to 60 million monthly transacting actives in September, and there is much more we plan to do to connect local communities in the future. We\u2019re using bitcoin and stablecoins as an enabler of Cash App\u2019s expansion, and have begun externalizing our lending infrastructure through Cash App Score.1 Q3\u201926 Highlights2\nGross Profit $3.24B +22% YoY Growth\nCash App Gross Profit $2.07B +27% YoY Growth\nSquare Gross Profit $1.16B +14% YoY Growth\nOperating Income $428M 13% Margin +5% YoY Growth3\nAdjusted\nOperating Income $923M 29% Margin +92% YoY Growth4\nDiluted Net Income Per Share (\u201cEPS\u201d) $0.92 +24% YoY Growth\nAdjusted Diluted EPS $1.13 +109% YoY Growth5\n"}
{"page": 2, "text": "1 A transacting active is a Cash App account that has at least one financial transaction using any product or service within Cash App during a specified period. A transacting active for a specific Cash App product has at least one financial transaction using that product during the specified period and is referred to as an active. Examples of transactions include sending or receiving a peer-to-peer payment, transferring money into or out of Cash App, making a purchase using Cash App Card, earning a dividend on a stock investment, and paying back a loan, among others. Certain of these accounts may share an alias identifier with one or more other transacting active accounts. This could represent, among other things, one customer with multiple accounts or multiple customers sharing one alias identifier (for example, families). 2 Reconciliations of non-GAAP financial measures used in this letter to their nearest GAAP equivalents are provided at the end of this letter. Please see these reconciliations for additional detail and a description of certain items that affected operating income (loss) and net income (loss) in the third quarter of 2026. 3 Margins are all calculated as a percent of gross profit. 4 Adjusted Operating Income is a non-GAAP measure of operating performance and the profitability of our business, fully burdened by share-based compensation. For more information, please refer to the \u201cKey Operating Metrics and Non-GAAP Financial Measures\u201d section of this letter. 5 Adjusted Diluted EPS is a non-GAAP measure of profitability of our business. For more information, please refer to the \u201cKey Operating Metrics and Non-GAAP Financial Measures\u201d section of this letter. 1\nBut what does it mean to \"make our company an intelligence, and enable others to do the same\"? We started down this path with goose, which helped us automate a lot of mundane engineering tasks earlier than most other companies, and which quickly evolved into a tool we could use for any role or task. It's now deeply embedded in our company's foundation, and enables us to help realize our vision to turn our company into a working intelligence, a mini AGI. We focused on separating our work into four layers: capabilities (the jobs we can do), interfaces (our distribution, from apps to hardware to payment wands), world models (the deep real-time understanding of both our customers and company), and proactive intelligence (a predictive engine to anticipate future needs, both for our customers and us).\n"}
{"page": 3, "text": "Buzz is probably the greatest manifestation of the \"Block as Intelligence\" vision. It's an environment for building and collaborating that brings all of our ideas together into one surface. It's really great for solo work, and when someone is ready, even better for team work. It's designed to scale from a company of our size, to one much larger, and even to our smallest sellers (Squaremates is built on Buzz to offer our sellers best-in-class intelligence and team collaboration). It's model agnostic, entirely self-sovereign (meaning you can run it privately on your own network or system), and starts to reduce the dependency on tools like Slack, Discord, and GitHub.\nWe have so much confidence in Buzz and our related tools that we recently created a new ecosystem called Builderlab. Block was Builderlab\u2019s first customer, but the problems we\u2019re solving are not unique to us. Companies that want to transform how they work at scale need more than a model. They need simple, accessible interfaces, connections between existing tools, and secure ways to manage and evaluate the work agentic intelligence does. We built Builderlab to help transform how Block works, and our goal is to bring it to other companies to help them do the same.\nIn October we began piloting Buzz with prospective customers, and we expect to generate our first machine intelligence revenue in the fourth quarter. We have more to prove as we build a product offering and go-to-market strategy for an entirely new customer base, but we\u2019re excited to extend what we\u2019ve built for ourselves to others. We\u2019ll approach this new ecosystem with the same principles that have guided us since the beginning: start small, give it soul, give time back, make it better. We're hosting an event in San Francisco on November 10 to properly introduce Builderlab.\nFinally, we believe voice is the most natural way to interact with intelligence, so we created Dessa, which we view as the most authentic full-duplex speech model ever built. Talking with it feels like talking with a person\u2026full of emotion, nuance, pauses, and a natural cadence. Dessa truly pushes the frontier of deep learning models and is a significant contribution to the field. I encourage you to listen to the samples we've shared, and expect more from us in the future on how we plan to apply this groundbreaking work to our capabilities.\nWe believe we've meaningfully crossed the chasm of transforming our company from one that creates traditional financial products into one that streams intelligence as people need it. Thank you for your support along the way, and thank you for investing in our future.\n2\n"}
{"page": 4, "text": "Business Highlights Make our company an intelligence and enable others to do the same The AI tools we\u2019ve built are giving us time back and helping accelerate product velocity. In October, we began rolling out model routing to all employees at Block, giving everyone access to a simple, efficient way to match their work to the right model capability. In recent weeks, AI has been autonomously reviewing and merging an increasing number of our production code changes related to low-risk, routine work so our teams can focus more of their time on building differentiated products for customers. Our focus on velocity is delivering more value to our customers faster: through the first three quarters of the year, we've released over 180 new Square features, already more than in 2024 and 2025 combined.\nWe\u2019re focusing more development effort on externalizing these capabilities so others can move faster too. Buzz makes AI multiplayer and serves as a communication and code repository tool accessible to everyone. We've shipped more than two dozen releases to Buzz since launching our alpha release in July. We\u2019ve rebuilt onboarding so a new user opens the app directly into a live team of AI agents already at work, with their existing developer tools detected automatically rather than wired up by hand. We\u2019ve also released the governance and identity infrastructure, including an admin console, encrypted identity backups, and centralized community moderation that enterprises need to deploy Buzz at scale.\nBuild the best intelligence to run your business Square GPV growth accelerated to 14% in the third quarter, the strongest growth rate since the first quarter of 2023, driven by our continued distribution expansion and increased product velocity.6 Self-onboarding continued to drive strong new customer acquisition while the number of new sellers joining Square through Independent Sales Organization (ISO) partners grew 90% quarter over quarter. Our field sales team and expanded enterprise motion continued to drive new customer acquisition, both in the U.S. and internationally, including Tommy\u2019s Tavern + Tap, a fast-scaling full-service restaurant chain that chose Square to run its 16-plus locations across four states, and Public House Group, the popular London hospitality group behind The Pelican and The Bull in Charlbury.\n"}
{"page": 5, "text": "In October, we launched Squaremates, our rebranded AI product for Square sellers that builds on the heritage of Square AI and Managerbot. Squaremates is generally available in the U.S. and brings some of Block\u2019s most powerful AI capabilities directly to Square sellers. We\u2019ve redesigned the experience to offer what we think is a leading AI product for businesses, grounded in the unique commerce, customer, appointment, and inventory data of each seller. Squaremates accesses cloud compute to take on more complex, multi-step work, such as staff analyses, drafting customer win-back campaigns and holiday promotions, and preparing purchase orders for food and beverage sellers. We plan to keep expanding Squaremates\u2019 capabilities, including an Apps platform that lets sellers build bespoke apps connected to their Square data and existing staff permissions, and procurement agents that help sellers manage inventory and order from suppliers. We also plan to bring Buzz into Squaremates, providing sellers with a team communication and task management platform built with AI agents as first-party participants.\nWe also continue to innovate on hardware with the October launch of the new Square Kiosk, our next-generation self-ordering device built from the ground up around voice-enabled AI. Customers can now order by voice or touch, ask questions about the menu, and get personalized recommendations, the same way they would at the counter. Kiosk was built with larger sellers in mind, and early response from multi-unit operators has been strong. We\u2019ve seen franchise operators who started out skeptical of kiosks now wanting to place Square Kiosks throughout their national footprint. We set out to build hardware that saves sellers time and money while helping them serve more customers, and we believe we've delivered on that vision.\nBuild the best intelligence to run your money Cash App reached 60 million monthly transacting actives in September with Neighborhoods playing a key role in actives growth this quarter. We also launched Cash App en Espa\u00f1ol to better serve Spanish-speaking customers, and we continued to expand Families product functionality to help us reach the next generation. In the third quarter, we\n6 Square GPV is defined as the total dollar amount of all card and bank payments processed by sellers using Square, net of refunds. 3\nmade the flow from a managed Cash App Account for Kids to a sponsored teen account smoother so customers can keep growing with Cash App as their financial needs evolve.\n"}
{"page": 6, "text": "In the third quarter, we continued to focus our building and partnership efforts on better serving modern earners, the estimated 70% of Cash App monthly transacting actives who earn income through a combination of freelancing, content creation, and hourly or gig work. We launched our pilot of Earn in P2P, a no-fee way to accept payments and track more of your earnings easily in Cash App. In September, we launched a partnership with Workday that lets eligible employees choose Cash App for direct deposit during payroll onboarding, and in October we announced a partnership with UKG to join their Frontline Worker Network. We\u2019re also expanding the ways platforms can pay earners directly into Cash App, and we\u2019ve begun testing ways for customers to access their pay as they earn it, rather than waiting for payday, whether or not their employer works with us.\nCash App Tags became generally available in August, and we're seeing this new hardware deepen engagement with Cash App. Customers who ordered a Tag before September spent more in the month after their order than in the month before, with the largest increase among those who had been less active users of Cash App Card. Since general availability in August, one in five Tag orderers is a new or re-engaged Card active. We plan to continue to expand the Cash App Tags platform with new form factors and partnerships while continuing to execute on our broader commerce network strategy. In the third quarter, Fanatics chose Afterpay as its preferred buy now, pay later partner, and Afterpay Pay Monthly launched in Australia, with Apple as our flagship launch partner. Cash App Pay momentum also continued as Roblox added Cash App Pay functionality and we signed distribution partnerships with Checkout.com, Fiserv, and Finix.\nBuild open financial infrastructure for all For the past six years, we've used our credit infrastructure and underwriting expertise to deliver liquidity solutions to Cash App customers. Now we're opening our capabilities up to others. In September, we announced that Cash App Score will be made available to lenders through Nova Credit, our first step in bringing Cash App's underwriting to more use cases. We expect to sign more partnerships in the future.\n"}
{"page": 7, "text": "Connect neighborhoods globally In August, we auto enrolled over 30,000 new seller locations onto Neighborhoods, our loyalty, discovery, and rewards experience that connects Square sellers with consumers, growing the Neighborhoods network over 10x from June. As of September, sellers on Neighborhoods represent nearly $5 billion in annualized GPV, up nearly 5x since June, and Cash App actives that have engaged with Neighborhoods grew over 5x in September compared to June. We continued to see strong per-seller engagement. Sellers that have been live on Neighborhoods for over a year averaged over 1,000 followers, with 10% of their GPV on Neighborhoods. We plan to continue expanding the Neighborhoods network further while continuing to innovate on the product to make Neighborhoods more valuable to sellers and their customers. We\u2019re also focused on increasing the connectivity between Neighborhoods and Square products like Loyalty and Square Online as well as expanding Neighborhoods functionality across more Square hardware, building on early rollouts on Square Stand and Reader.\n4\n2027 Preliminary Outlook We expect Block to perform meaningfully better in 2026 than the expectations we outlined at Investor Day in November 2025.7 In 2027, we expect momentum to continue, with Block gross profit expected to grow at least 15% year over year as our investments in AI and our broader focus on velocity drive sustained growth at scale. We expect Adjusted Operating Income to grow 30% year over year to $4.575 billion as we expand margins while still investing meaningfully to sustain long-term growth. In Square, our investments in distribution expansion are beginning to compound, and we expect to accelerate GPV growth for the third consecutive year, with gross profit growing roughly in line with GPV. In Cash App, we expect at least low-single-digit actives growth and strong growth in both Commerce Enablement and Financial Solutions gross profit.8 Neighborhoods has started to connect Square and Cash App, while our investments in AI are laying the foundation for what we believe will become Block\u2019s next ecosystem.\n2026 2027\nNov 2025 Investor Day Nov 2026 Guidance \u0394 Nov 2025 Investor Day Nov 2026 Prelim. Outlook \u0394\nGross Profit ($B) $11.980 $12.626 +5% $13.770 $14.519 +5%\nYoY Growth 17% 22%\nMid-teens 15%\nAdjusted Operating Income ($B) $2.700 $3.519 +30% $3.510 $4.575 +30%\nYoY Growth 31% 69%\n30% 30%\nAdjusted Operating Income Margin (%) of gross profit 23% 28%\n25% 32%\n"}
{"page": 8, "text": "Square Gaining share in the U.S. and expanding further in international markets We estimate more than half of Square\u2019s addressable market in the U.S. is still using legacy incumbent solutions. In 2026, we\u2019ve expanded product functionality and ramped investments across every distribution channel we operate in to serve even more customers in this massive market. Year to date through August, self-onboarded new volume added has grown at the best rate in over a decade.9 We\u2019ve continued to scale field sales, and we now have over 400 ISO partners onboarded. We\u2019ll continue to invest in compounding these growth curves in 2027.\nOutside the U.S., the share of sellers on legacy solutions is even greater, and we've grown faster. We've delivered over 20% constant currency GPV growth in each quarter for the past six years, and we expect to sustain that strength in 2027. We've signed ISO partnerships in the U.K., Canada, Australia, Ireland, France, and Spain. We\u2019re hiring field sales across several markets, and we\u2019re exploring partnerships that could transform the scale of our business in select countries.\n7 We have not provided the forward-looking GAAP equivalents for certain forward-looking non-GAAP metrics, including Adjusted Operating Income (Loss) and Adjusted Diluted EPS, or GAAP reconciliations of any of the aforementioned, as a result of the uncertainty regarding, and the potential variability of, reconciling items such as contingencies, restructuring, and other charges. Accordingly, the Company has relied upon the exception in item 10(e)(1)(i)(B) of Regulation S-K to exclude such reconciliations, as the reconciliations of these non-GAAP guidance metrics to their corresponding GAAP equivalents are not available without unreasonable effort. However, it is important to note that material changes to reconciling items could have a significant effect on future GAAP results. We have provided reconciliations of other historical GAAP to non-GAAP metrics in tables at the end of this letter, as well as relevant non-GAAP definitions. 8 For definitions of Cash App Commerce Enablement and Financial Solutions, see the Block Financial Metrics section on page 9 of this letter. 9 New Volume Added (NVA) is the total gross payment volume (GPV) processed, or expected to be processed, by new sellers during their first 12 months on Square. While intended to represent incremental volume from new cohorts, it may also include GPV from existing sellers in cases such as new locations or event-based merchant tokens. For the purpose of this letter, figures exclude deactivated merchants. 5\n"}
{"page": 9, "text": "Square GPV growth translating to gross profit growth As of September, only 16% of our U.S. sellers use monetized software, and we see opportunities to innovate on pricing and packaging to drive software attach higher next year. Squaremates is now generally available in the U.S. and in 2027 we plan to scale its automation capabilities further to add more value to more sellers. Beyond AI, we believe no one can match Neighborhoods and our ability to deliver loyalty, marketing, and discovery solutions to sellers, on their terms, while enabling them to own their customer relationships.\nCash App We built Cash App into one of the most successful consumer fintechs in the world by doing two things well: developing unique ways to reach customers who want to try our product, and consistently delivering more value to them through product innovation. That is the entire growth algorithm, simple to say and brutally hard to execute. It is how we\u2019ve evolved from a P2P app into a full financial services app serving tens of millions of customers, and it is how we plan to sustain growth as Borrow growth normalizes. As of September, Cash App is now over $8 billion in annualized gross profit, with 60 million monthly transacting actives, the fourth largest debit program in the U.S., and over $75 billion in annualized consumer lending origination volume.10 We are still just getting started.\nWe have multiple initiatives to grow Cash App actives We are expanding the audiences we can serve with Cash App, and we believe Neighborhoods, Cash App Families, and Cash App global expansion will be the driving forces behind long-term Cash App actives growth. We believe that almost every American shops at a Square seller in a given year, and we will soon be able to reach them across all Square hardware devices through Neighborhoods.11 For new actives who join through Neighborhoods, we're planning multiple engagement strategies, starting with introducing them to Cash App's commerce products. Beyond Neighborhoods, we see meaningful opportunities to expand our Teens and Families products through a focus on enhancing the experience for sponsors, making it easier for teens to graduate accounts, and introducing products that reinforce healthy financial habits. We continue to focus on helping modern earners by establishing deeper primary banking relationships.12 We plan to make it easier to set up direct deposit, expand the ways customers can access their pay as they earn it, and evolve Cash App Green so customers unlock more benefits as they bring more of their financial lives to Cash App. The needs of modern earners aren\u2019t unique to people in the U.S., and in 2027 we plan to build more capabilities for a global audience.\n"}
{"page": 10, "text": "Growing Cash App Commerce Enablement through volume growth and monetization rate expansion13 As of September, 28 million monthly transacting actives used Cash App Card, which means 32 million did not. We have more ways to drive commerce attach rates than ever before, including new Cash App Tags form factors and partnerships, growing Cash App Pay and Afterpay merchant partnerships, and expanded Afterpay Pre-Purchase and Post-Purchase eligibility. We expect to serve more customers as our commerce product set expands, and we have meaningful room to expand our relationship with customers who already use our commerce products. The median American spends $1,650 a month on credit and debit cards. Our median commerce active spends $217 a month on Cash App, and we expect to continue to expand our monetization rate as we drive engagement higher: as of September, only 10% of Cash App Card and Cash App Pay actives use a BNPL product, and we see nearly 3x higher gross profit for actives who use BNPL compared with those who don't.\nBorrow growth driving Cash App Financial Solutions growth We believe we continue to have opportunities to expand Borrow limits and eligibility while sustaining strong variable profit margins and industry-leading returns on capital. As of September, the average Borrow limit was $300, compared to the highest limits we offer of $1,500. We expect to increase average limits over the next several years as more\n10 Consumer lending includes Cash App Borrow and our BNPL products. 11 Based on internal estimates comparing Census Vintage 2025 population estimates as of July 1, 2025 to the number of Square buyer profiles. Individuals may have more than one buyer profile. 12 Square and Cash App are financial services platforms, not banks. Throughout this letter, any reference to Square or Cash App\u02bcs banking offerings or terms such as \u201cprimary banking actives\u201d refer to products and services that are offered through Block\u02bcs Industrial Bank, Square Financial Services, Inc., or through our third-party bank partners. 13 Cash App Commerce Enablement Monetization Rate is calculated by dividing Cash App Commerce Enablement gross profit by Cash App Commerce Enablement volume. Cash App Commerce Enablement gross profit is primarily composed of Cash App Card and BNPL products. Cash App Commerce Enablement volume includes GPV from Cash App Card, Cash App Pay, BNPL products, and Cash App Business. 6\ncustomer cohorts mature and our proprietary data compounds. We also expect to generate revenue from externalizing Cash App Score to partners who value our underwriting infrastructure and want to extend credit to Cash App customers.\n"}
{"page": 11, "text": "Investing in long-term sustainable growth We view risk loss as an input to maximizing variable profit across our lending products.14 Since launching Square Loans in 2014, we've been able to hold loss rates consistent with our target while meaningfully expanding eligibility to more and more customers. Every annual Square loan cohort has performed in line with or better than our 4% loss target, and we\u2019ve been able to grow Square Loans at a 27% CAGR over the past decade because of our disciplined underwriting approach, proprietary data, and world-class expertise. We are taking the same approach, with the same competitive differentiators, with Cash App Consumer Lending. Over the past two years, Cash App Consumer Lending originations growth compounded at 45%. Cash App Consumer Lending variable profit compounded at nearly the same rate. We see meaningful ecosystem benefits beyond the returns we generate on our lending products: deeper engagement with Cash App Commerce functionality, higher primary banking attach rates, and ultimately higher gross profit per active, excluding the economics from Borrow.\n"}
{"page": 12, "text": "In 2027, we expect to ramp investments in several long-term strategic growth initiatives beyond lending, most notably scaling Neighborhoods and AI solutions externally. Our growth guidance does not rely on these initiatives, but we believe they can become meaningful drivers of durable growth, and we will scale them with the same discipline we apply to our core growth investments. Those core growth investments have delivered strong returns in 2026: year to date through August, we\u2019ve seen an estimated five-quarter payback for Square new volume added, with each of self-onboarding, field sales, and ISO distribution operating in line with or better than our return and payback investment thresholds.15 In Cash App, we\u2019ve seen less than a three-quarter payback on our performance marketing and referral investments.16 Beyond go-to-market, we\u2019ve expanded our ability to drive velocity with AI tooling in a thoughtful way, building evaluation techniques that help us understand model specific ROI on a wide range of engineering tasks. We plan to keep investing in increasing velocity in 2027, especially since many of these investments benefit our external AI products too. We believe the most valuable investments we can make are in sustaining long-term gross profit growth, which is where we are focused in 2027 as we drive toward 30% Adjusted Operating Income growth. 14 Variable profit is defined as gross profit less processing costs and risk losses, excluding financing costs. 15 Represents sellers onboarded through marketing-led self-onboarding (web and app), field sales, and third-party sales (resellers and ISOs). Square Payback Period measures the time it takes for the gross profit we expect sellers to generate to recover acquisition costs. It is calculated by dividing acquisition costs by the average quarterly gross profit sellers are expected to generate over their lifetime. Self-onboarding acquisition costs reflect Square Marketing Spend, which excludes personnel, travel and expenses, and learning and development costs. Field sales and third-party sales acquisition costs include personnel, overhead, hardware discounts, and partner bounties. 16 Represents paid marketing and referrals in the first nine months of 2026. Cash App Payback Period is customer acquisition cost per new or reactivated active divided by estimated quarterly variable profit per active, averaged across all Cash App customers active during the period. 7\nFinancial Discussion We exceeded our third quarter guidance for gross profit, Adjusted Operating Income, and Adjusted Diluted EPS. Gross profit grew 22% year over year to $3.24 billion, with strong growth in both Cash App and Square. Adjusted Operating Income grew 92% year over year to $923 million, with an all time high margin of 29%, and Adjusted Diluted EPS grew 109% year over year to a record $1.13.\nThird Quarter 2026 Financial Highlights\n"}
{"page": 13, "text": "Cash App Cash App monthly transacting actives grew to 60 million in September, as Neighborhoods brought more Square seller customers into Cash App. Cash App gross profit grew 27%, with strength across Financial Solutions and Commerce Enablement. Consumer Lending origination volume grew 40% year over year to $19.0 billion, driven by Cash App Borrow and Afterpay Post-Purchase.\nSquare In the third quarter, Square GPV grew 14% year over year on a reported and constant currency basis, with U.S. GPV growth accelerating to 10.4% year over year and International GPV growth sustaining strong performance, growing 26% year over year on a reported and constant currency basis. Square gross profit grew in line with GPV growth in the third quarter.\nProfitability We outperformed our profitability guidance in the third quarter, with record Adjusted Operating Income and Adjusted Diluted EPS. We also achieved record Adjusted EBITDA in the third quarter. Operating income was $428 million, up 5% year over year, while Adjusted Operating Income grew 92% year over year to $923 million. Net income attributable to common stockholders was $566 million and Adjusted EBITDA reached $1.2 billion. GAAP diluted EPS was $0.92, and Adjusted Diluted EPS grew 109% year over year to $1.13.\nGuidance\nQ4'26\n2026\nGross Profit $3.315B\nGross Profit $12.626B\nYoY Growth 15%\nYoY Growth 22%\nAdjusted Operating Income $1.005B\nAdjusted Operating Income $3.519B\n% Margin 30%\n% Margin 28%\nRule of X17 46%\nRule of X 50%\nAdjusted Diluted EPS $1.15\nAdjusted Diluted EPS $4.15\nYoY Growth 77%\nYoY Growth 75%\nWe are raising our full-year guidance to reflect the momentum we are seeing across Block. For 2026, we now expect $12.626 billion in gross profit, up 22% year over year, and Adjusted Operating Income of $3.519 billion, or 28% margin, growing 69% year over year. We also expect Adjusted Diluted EPS to grow 75% to $4.15.\n17 Rule of 40 is the sum of our gross profit growth and Adjusted Operating Income margin as a percent of gross profit. We may refer to a \u201cRule of\u201d number other than 40 to refer to the sum of gross profit growth and Adjusted Operating Income margin as a percent of gross profit for the period given. 8\nBlock Financial Metrics18\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26\nRevenue ($M) 6,115 6,252 6,057 6,618 6,719\nCommerce Enablement19 2,999 3,050 2,938 3,342 3,476\nFinancial Solutions20 1,095 1,222 1,322 1,382 1,440\nBitcoin Ecosystem21 2,021 1,980 1,796 1,894 1,804\nGross Profit ($M) 2,662 2,872 2,909 3,166 3,235\nYoY Growth 18% 24% 27% 25% 22%\nCommerce Enablement22 1,552 1,623 1,608 1,805 1,851\nYoY Growth 11% 11% 15% 18% 19%\nFinancial Solutions 1,006 1,132 1,233 1,289 1,332\nYoY Growth 34% 51% 55% 43% 32%\nBitcoin Ecosystem 104 118 68 72 52\nYoY Growth 8% 10% (26)% (31)% (50)%\n"}
{"page": 14, "text": "Overall Block gross profit grew 22% year over year in the third quarter, with 27% year-over-year growth in Cash App and 14% year-over-year growth in Square. Commerce Enablement gross profit growth accelerated to 19% year over year, driven by strong payments volume in Square and strength in Cash App. Financial Solutions gross profit grew 32% year over year, driven by Cash App Consumer Lending. Bitcoin Ecosystem gross profit declined 50% year over year, driven primarily by a strategic decision to reduce the fee we charge on certain bitcoin transactions on Cash App.\n18 Quarterly revenue and gross profit by category may not sum to totals due to rounding. 19 Commerce enablement is primarily comprised of transaction fees related to Square payments, software, and hardware, Cash App Card, Cash App Pay, the Company\u2019s BNPL products, Cash App Business accounts, TIDAL, and various other software as a service (\u201cSaaS\u201d) products. 20 Financial solutions is primarily comprised of Cash App Borrow, Cash App Instant Deposit, ATM withdrawal fees, interest earned on customer funds, and Square Loans. 21 Bitcoin ecosystem primarily consists of customer purchases of bitcoin within Cash App, Proto, and bitcoin withdrawal fees. 22 Commerce Enablement gross profit reflects the impact of amortization of acquired technology assets. 9\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26\nGross Profit ($M) 2,662 2,872 2,909 3,166 3,235\nYoY Growth 18% 24% 27% 25% 22%\nOperating Income (Loss) ($M) 409 485 (172) 447 428\nOperating Income (Loss) Margin (%) of gross profit 15% 17% (6)% 14% 13%\nAdjusted Operating Income ($M) 480 588 728 864 923\nAdjusted Operating Income Margin (%) of gross profit 18% 20% 25% 27% 29%\nDiluted Net Income (Loss) Per Share (\"EPS\") ($) 0.74 0.19 (0.52) 0.15 0.92\nAdjusted Diluted EPS ($) 0.54 0.65 0.85 1.02 1.13\nStrong gross profit growth and Adjusted Operating Income margin expansion compounded into meaningful Adjusted Diluted EPS growth in the third quarter. On a GAAP basis, we generated $428 million in operating income in the third quarter of 2026, compared to $409 million in the third quarter of 2025. Adjusted Operating Income grew 92% year over year as margin expanded more than 10 percentage points to 29%, supported by strong gross profit growth and disciplined execution. On a GAAP basis, we delivered diluted EPS of $0.92, an increase of 24% year over year. Adjusted Diluted EPS grew 109% year over year to $1.13.\n10\nCash App\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26\nCash App Gross Profit ($M) 1,624 1,831 1,908 1,973 2,070\nYoY Growth 24% 33% 38% 31% 27%\nCash App Operating Metrics\nCash App Monthly Transacting Actives (M) 58 59 59 59 60\nYoY Growth 2% 3% 4% 3% 4%\nCash App Primary Banking Actives (M)23 8.3 9.3 9.7 9.4 9.4\nYoY Growth 18% 22% 18% 17% 13%\nCommerce Enablement Volume ($B) 49.7 54.7 55.0 56.5 56.9\nYoY Growth 17% 17% 18% 17% 14%\nCommerce Enablement Monetization Rate 1.56% 1.61% 1.61% 1.65% 1.74%\nConsumer Lending Origination Volume ($B) 13.6 18.5 17.6 18.9 19.0\n"}
{"page": 15, "text": "YoY Growth 51% 69% 82% 59% 40%\nTotal Cash App Inflows ($B)24 79 83 88 87 86\nYoY Growth 12% 15% 14% 13% 10%\nInflows Per Transacting Active ($)25 1,366 1,410 1,494 1,469 1,446\nYoY Growth 10% 12% 10% 9% 6%\nCash App gross profit grew 27% year over year with strength in both Financial Solutions and Commerce Enablement. Cash App monthly transacting actives reached 60 million in September, and Neighborhoods was a key contributor to actives growth. We believe the ability to grow actives through the Square ecosystem is one of Cash App\u2019s strongest differentiators.\nCommerce Enablement gross profit growth was driven by Cash App Card and BNPL products, led by Afterpay Post-Purchase. Commerce Enablement volume grew 14% year over year to $56.9 billion, driven by Cash App Card and Afterpay BNPL. Commerce Enablement monetization rate expanded 18 basis points year over year to 1.74%, driven by higher BNPL adoption on Cash App Card through Afterpay Post-Purchase and Pre-Purchase. Financial Solutions gross profit growth was driven primarily by Cash App Borrow.\nConsumer Lending origination volume grew 40% year over year to $19.0 billion, led by Cash App Borrow and Afterpay Post-Purchase. Afterpay Post-Purchase origination volume more than tripled year over year, and Afterpay Pre-Purchase origination volume more than tripled quarter over quarter in its first full quarter of general availability. As of the end of September, we completed the migration of Cash App Borrow to a six-week loan construct. During this 23 A Primary Banking Active (PBA) is a Cash App account that receives inflows from ACH or certain original credit transactions relating to earned wages, excluding tax refunds and ACH transfers, or spent at least $500 per month across Cash App, including Cash App Card, Cash App Pay, Afterpay through Cash App, and ACH bill pay during a specified period. 24 Historically, our Cash App ecosystem has experienced improvements in revenue, gross profit, and inflows related to the distribution of government funds as customers have deposited more funds into Cash App during these times, including during the first quarter when U.S. tax refunds are typically distributed. 25 Inflows per transacting active refers to total inflows in the quarter divided by monthly actives for the last month of the quarter. Inflows refers to funds entering the Cash App ecosystem. Inflows does not include the movement of funds when funds remain in the Cash App ecosystem or when funds leave the Cash App ecosystem, or inflows related to the Afterpay app. 11\nproduct construct shift, we held Borrow limits constant, which had the effect of lowering Borrow originations per customer by 7% quarter over quarter. With the shift to a six-week Borrow construct now complete, we expect to begin expanding Borrow limits again in the near term. More repayment flexibility was a top customer request for Borrow, and we\u2019re excited to deliver that functionality for customers by aligning terms with our BNPL products.\n"}
{"page": 16, "text": "Square\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26\nSquare Gross Profit ($M) 1,018 993 982 1,160 1,157\nYoY Growth 9% 7% 9% 13% 14%\nTotal Square GPV ($M) 67,151 64,960 61,209 72,848 76,359\nYoY Growth 12% 10% 13% 13% 14%\nConstant Currency (\"CC\") GPV YoY Growth 12% 10% 11% 13% 14%\nSquare U.S. GPV\nYoY Growth 8.9% 7.0% 8.2% 9.8% 10%\n% of Total Square GPV 79% 78% 78% 78% 77%\nSquare International GPV\nYoY Growth 26% 24% 35% 28% 26%\nCC GPV YoY Growth 25% 25% 26% 25% 26%\n% of Total Square GPV 21% 22% 22% 22% 23%\nSquare GPV grew 14% year over year in the third quarter on a reported and constant currency basis to $76.4 billion, reflecting strong GPV growth from new sellers. GPV from food and beverage sellers was up 20% year over year, driven by strength in U.S. food and beverage GPV. GPV from retail sellers and services sellers grew 13% and 8% year over year, respectively. We continued to see the fastest year-over-year growth in our mid-market seller segment (>$500K in annualized GPV) compared to our other seller segments during the third quarter.\nSquare gross profit grew 14% year over year in the third quarter in line with Square GPV growth. Growth was driven primarily by Commerce Enablement, reflecting stronger payments volume and increased software adoption, supported by the simplified pricing and packaging options we introduced last year. We also saw continued momentum in Financial Solutions, driven by Square Loans.26 We continue to expect Square gross profit to grow roughly in line with Square GPV for the remainder of the year.\n26 For definitions of Square Commerce Enablement and Financial Solutions, see the Block Financial Metrics section on page 9 of this letter. 12\nOperating Expenses and Non-GAAP Operating Expenses ($M)\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26\nOperating Expenses ($M) 2,252 2,387 3,081 2,719 2,807\nRestructuring Share-Based Compensation 2 \u2013 110 4 13\nAmortization of Customer and Other Acquired Intangible Assets 34 34 34 34 34\nAcquisition-Related and Integration Costs 0 0 0 0 0\nContingencies, Restructuring and Other Charges 21 54 743 365 435\nNon-GAAP Operating Expenses ($M) 2,195 2,298 2,194 2,315 2,325\nIn the third quarter, product development expenses decreased 11% year over year on a GAAP basis and decreased 12% on a non-GAAP basis, driven by reduced personnel-related costs associated with the organizational changes we executed in February. Sales and marketing expenses grew 15% year over year on a GAAP basis and grew 14% on a non-GAAP basis, driven by an increase in go-to-market investments. General and administrative expenses were up 56% year over year on a GAAP basis, primarily driven by increased accrued legal contingencies, and down 21% year over year on a non-GAAP basis. For more information on accrued legal contingencies, please see the Commitments and Contingencies footnote in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026.\n"}
{"page": 17, "text": "Transaction, loan, and consumer receivable losses increased 66% year over year on a GAAP basis, driven by growth in loan volumes and product mix shifts. Third quarter Cash App Consumer Lending variable profit margin was in line with the third quarter of 2025 and improved modestly quarter over quarter, demonstrating strong returns and underwriting as originations continued to grow.27 Borrow risk loss rates remained healthy as we completed our migration to six-week loans in September. We expect year-over-year growth in transaction, loan, and consumer receivable losses to moderate further in the fourth quarter.\n27 Consumer Lending variable profit margin is Consumer Lending variable profit divided by Consumer Lending originations. We define variable profit as gross profit less processing costs and risk losses, excluding financing costs. 13\nKey Profitability Measures and EPS ($M, except per share figures)\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26\nOperating Income (Loss) 409 485 (172) 447 428\nAdjusted Operating Income 480 588 728 864 923\nNet Income (Loss) attributable to common stockholders 462 116 (309) 89 566\nAdjusted Net Income 337 402 513 620 697\nAdjusted EBITDA 833 930 1,010 1,169 1,228\nWeighted-average shares used to compute Diluted EPS 622 614 598 609 615\nWeighted-average shares used to compute Adjusted Diluted EPS 622 616 604 609 615\nDiluted EPS ($) 0.74 0.19 (0.52) 0.15 0.92\nAdjusted Diluted EPS ($) 0.54 0.65 0.85 1.02 1.13\n14\nNon-GAAP Cash Flow ($M)\nQ3'25 Q4'25 Q1'26 Q2'26 Q3'26 TRAILING 12 MONTHS28\nNet cash provided by operating activities 1,451 621 966 1,020 1,016 3,623\nLess: Purchase of property and equipment (51) (41) (31) (53) (44) (169)\nFree Cash Flow 1,400 580 935 967 972 3,453\nReversal of:\nChanges in settlements receivable 33 196 (90) 235 129 470\nChanges in customers payable 3 (61) 122 (164) (268) (370)\nChanges in settlements payable \u2013 \u2013 (2) 2 \u2013 \u2013\nSales, principal payments and forgiveness of PPP loans 